# Trading / brokerage back-office operations — 18 tasks. THIS FILE IS DATA.
#
# Same contract as mixed.jsonl: one JSON object per line, {"id", "task",
# "expectation", "difficulty"}. "expectation" is a success criterion for the
# generic rubric judge, NOT an answer key any code matches on.
#
# Why this workload. It is the request mix a broker's operations desk actually
# puts to an assistant: order-book arithmetic, charge computation, margin
# sufficiency, corporate-action adjustment, expiry-calendar rules, penalty
# slabs, position P&L, execution-quality maths, file reconciliation, and a
# small number of genuinely open judgement calls.
#
# Every task is SELF-CONTAINED. Where a rule could change with regulation
# (settlement cycle, expiry-shift rule, penalty slabs) the rule is stated inside
# the prompt and the task is to apply it. Nothing here needs live market data, a
# tool, web access, or recall of what a circular says this quarter — so the same
# file scores the same way next year, and a wrong answer is a reasoning failure
# rather than a stale-knowledge failure.
#
#   python proofs/p8_policy_tradeops.py --tasks proofs/tasks/tradeops.jsonl
{"id": "to01_spread", "difficulty": "trivial", "task": "In an equity order book the best bid is 250.10 and the best ask is 250.35, both in rupees. State the bid-ask spread in rupees. Give the number only.", "expectation": "States 0.25 (rupees). Any answer other than 0.25 fails."}
{"id": "to02_lots", "difficulty": "trivial", "task": "A futures contract on a stock has a lot size of 25 shares. A trader wants exposure to exactly 300 underlying shares. How many lots must they trade? Give the number only.", "expectation": "States 12 lots."}
{"id": "to03_settlement", "difficulty": "trivial", "task": "Cash-segment equity trades settle on a T+1 basis, where T is the trade date and settlement happens on the next trading day. A trade is executed on Monday 6 April 2026. Both 6 and 7 April 2026 are normal trading and settlement days. On what date does the trade settle, and what weekday is that?", "expectation": "States 7 April 2026 and identifies it as a Tuesday."}
{"id": "to04_reject_json", "difficulty": "trivial", "task": "Return a single JSON object, and nothing else, describing a rejected order. It must have exactly four keys: \"order_id\" set to the string \"ORD-7781\", \"status\" set to the string \"REJECTED\", \"reason_code\" set to the string \"RMS_MARGIN_SHORTFALL\", and \"shortfall_inr\" set to the number 12500.50.", "expectation": "Output is one JSON object with exactly those four keys and those exact values, shortfall_inr as a number rather than a string, with no extra keys and no surrounding prose or code fences."}
{"id": "to05_brokerage", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "A broker charges 0.03% of turnover per executed order as brokerage, capped at 20 rupees per order. A client places one intraday equity trade: buys 100 shares at 1,200 rupees, then sells the same 100 shares at 1,215 rupees. Each leg is one order. What is the total brokerage across both legs, in rupees?", "expectation": "States 40 rupees in total, having applied the 20-rupee cap to each of the two legs (0.03% would be 36 and 36.45 uncapped)."}
{"id": "to06_stt", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "Securities transaction tax on an intraday equity trade is charged at 0.025% of the sell-side turnover only. A client sells 500 shares at 840 rupees each. What is the STT, in rupees?", "expectation": "States 105 rupees, computed on the sell turnover of 420,000."}
{"id": "to07_margin_lots", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "A futures contract requires a SPAN margin of 58,000 rupees and an exposure margin of 22,000 rupees per lot. A client has 5,00,000 rupees of free cash and no existing positions. What is the maximum whole number of lots the client can carry, and how much free cash is left after taking that position?", "expectation": "States 6 lots and 20,000 rupees of cash remaining, using a total margin of 80,000 rupees per lot."}
{"id": "to08_bonus", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "A company announces a 1:1 bonus issue, meaning one additional share for each share held. A client holds 200 shares and the price before adjustment is 960 rupees. On the ex-bonus date the position is adjusted so the total value is unchanged. State the adjusted holding quantity and the adjusted price per share.", "expectation": "States 400 shares at 480 rupees per share."}
{"id": "to09_avg_price", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "A client accumulates one holding in three fills: 100 shares at 250 rupees, 200 shares at 262 rupees, and 150 shares at 245 rupees. What is the weighted average buy price per share, in rupees, rounded to two decimal places?", "expectation": "States 253.67 rupees per share (114,150 rupees across 450 shares). A simple mean of the three prices, 252.33, fails."}
{"id": "to10_circuit", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "A stock has a previous close of 480 rupees and a daily price band of 10% either side of that close. State the upper circuit price and the lower circuit price, in rupees.", "expectation": "States an upper circuit of 528 and a lower circuit of 432."}
{"id": "to11_short_pnl", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "A client shorts 300 shares at 1,450 rupees. Later the position is covered in two parts: 200 shares bought back at 1,410 rupees, and the remaining 100 shares bought back at 1,475 rupees. Ignoring all charges, what is the net realised profit or loss in rupees? State whether it is a profit or a loss.", "expectation": "States a net profit of 5,500 rupees (a 8,000 rupee gain on the first 200 shares, less a 2,500 rupee loss on the last 100)."}
{"id": "to12_expiry_shift", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "Weekly index option contracts expire on Thursday. If that Thursday is a trading holiday, the contract expires on the immediately preceding trading day instead. Thursday 14 May 2026 has been declared a trading holiday. Monday 11, Tuesday 12 and Wednesday 13 May 2026 are all normal trading days. On what date does that week's contract expire, and what weekday is it?", "expectation": "States 13 May 2026 and identifies it as a Wednesday."}
{"id": "to13_penalty_slab", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "A margin-shortfall penalty is levied daily under these rules: if the shortfall is less than 1,00,000 rupees AND less than 10% of the applicable margin, the penalty is 0.5% of the shortfall; in every other case the penalty is 1.0% of the shortfall. On a given day a client's applicable margin is 8,00,000 rupees and the shortfall is 90,000 rupees. What is the penalty for that day, in rupees? Show which slab applies.", "expectation": "States 900 rupees, identifying that the 1.0% slab applies because although 90,000 is under 1,00,000 it is 11.25% of the applicable margin, which is not below 10%. An answer of 450 rupees applies the wrong slab and fails."}
{"id": "to14_option_payoff", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "A client buys one lot of an index call option: lot size 50, strike 24,000, premium paid 120 per unit. At expiry the index settles at 24,180. Ignoring all charges, what is the net profit or loss on the position, in rupees?", "expectation": "States a net profit of 3,000 rupees: intrinsic value 180 per unit less 120 premium, times 50 units."}
{"id": "to15_breakeven", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "A desk routes each support request either to an expensive model that resolves it in one call costing 0.0012 dollars, or to a cheap model costing 0.00015 dollars per call, where a request the cheap model fails consumes 3 cheap calls in total before it is given up on. Assume a resolved request on the cheap route takes one call. Below what first-attempt resolution rate does the cheap route cost more per resolved request than the expensive route? Give the rate as a percentage.", "expectation": "States 30% (0.30). The price ratio is 8 and a failure consumes 3 calls, so the break-even rate is 3/(8-1+3). An answer near 12.5% or 37.5% fails."}
{"id": "to16_reconciliation", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "An internal trade file and the exchange trade file are being reconciled for one client. Internal file: T1 BUY 500 INFY, T2 SELL 300 TCS, T3 BUY 250 HDFCBANK, T4 SELL 400 ITC. Exchange file: T1 BUY 500 INFY, T2 SELL 300 TCS, T3 BUY 200 HDFCBANK, T4 SELL 400 ITC. Identify which trade does not reconcile, state the quantity difference, and state whether the internal file is over-stated or under-stated on that trade.", "expectation": "Identifies T3 (HDFCBANK) as the break, states a difference of 50 shares, and says the internal file is over-stated (250 internal against 200 at the exchange)."}
{"id": "to17_vwap_slippage", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "A parent order to buy 1,000 shares is filled in three child fills: 200 at 101.50, 300 at 102.00, and 500 at 102.40 rupees. The arrival price when the parent order was released was 101.80 rupees. State the execution VWAP to two decimal places, the slippage per share against the arrival price in rupees, and the total slippage cost in rupees for the whole order.", "expectation": "States a VWAP of 102.10, slippage of 0.30 rupees per share against the arrival price, and a total slippage cost of 300 rupees. The slippage must be reported as an adverse cost for a buy order, not a gain."}
{"id": "to18_route_judgement", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "An operations desk wants to automate the first draft of margin-shortfall notification emails to clients. In at most 150 words: state whether this workload should be routed to a cheap model or an expensive one, name the single metric that should decide it, and give one concrete condition under which your recommendation would flip.", "expectation": "Commits to one recommendation rather than surveying both; names cost per resolved (or completed) task, not cost per call or per token, as the deciding metric; and gives a concrete, checkable flip condition such as a first-attempt acceptance rate falling below a stated level, or the notification carrying a figure whose error is expensive to correct. Stays within roughly 150 words."}
